        "id": 3499867,
        "postDate": "2026-07-18T05:58:30.563Z",
        "rawMarkdown": "As it is gonna fix, then it would be now safe to tell what's actually found? I am not aware yet.",
        "content": "<p>As it is gonna fix, then it would be now safe to tell what's actually found? I am not aware yet.</p>",
        "replies": [
          {
            "id": 3500056,
            "postDate": "2026-07-18T12:26:15.307Z",
            "rawMarkdown": "(My current third place exploits the metric hack as I tested it last night.)\n\n**What the division-metric exploit was**\n\nThe competition score is `adjusted_edge_jaccard + 0.1 \u00d7 division_jaccard`. The exploit targeted the second term \u2014 the division Jaccard \u2014 which carries up to 0.1 of the total.\n\nThe division score works by pulling out \"division\" events (a parent node with two children) from your predicted lineage graph and matching them against the ground-truth divisions. In the pre-patch version, that matching relied on **weakly-connected-component** structure and did not strictly require each matched division to be a genuine parent\u2192two-daughter fork sitting near a real GT division.\n\nThe exploit added a small amount of synthetic graph structure **far outside the image volume**:\n\n- a single \"hub\" node placed at coordinates far outside the data (e.g. \u221210000, \u221210000, \u221210000), with edges to the root of every (or the largest *N*) track components \u2014 this merges the whole prediction into one big weakly-connected component;\n- a handful of synthetic \"fork\" chains (a divider node with two children) at those same far-away coordinates;\n- and dropping the real forks from the tracks.\n\nUnder the old matching this pushed the division Jaccard from ~0 to ~1.0, adding close to the full +0.1 to the score \u2014 even though none of the added nodes or edges correspond to a real cell or a real division. It's a pure scoring artifact; it does nothing for actual tracking quality.\n\n**The patch** (now public in the repo) requires divisions to be genuine **strongly-connected** parent\u2192two-daughter structures matched within the 7 \u00b5m distance radius. The far-away synthetic forks now register as false positives instead of true positives, which removes the benefit and is why exploiting submissions will drop on re-score.\n\n",
            "content": "<p>(My current third place exploits the metric hack as I tested it last night.)</p>\n<p><strong>What the division-metric exploit was</strong></p>\n<p>The competition score is <code>adjusted_edge_jaccard + 0.1 \u00d7 division_jaccard</code>. The exploit targeted the second term \u2014 the division Jaccard \u2014 which carries up to 0.1 of the total.</p>\n<p>The division score works by pulling out \"division\" events (a parent node with two children) from your predicted lineage graph and matching them against the ground-truth divisions. In the pre-patch version, that matching relied on <strong>weakly-connected-component</strong> structure and did not strictly require each matched division to be a genuine parent\u2192two-daughter fork sitting near a real GT division.</p>\n<p>The exploit added a small amount of synthetic graph structure <strong>far outside the image volume</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>a single \"hub\" node placed at coordinates far outside the data (e.g. \u221210000, \u221210000, \u221210000), with edges to the root of every (or the largest <em>N</em>) track components \u2014 this merges the whole prediction into one big weakly-connected component;</li>\n<li>a handful of synthetic \"fork\" chains (a divider node with two children) at those same far-away coordinates;</li>\n<li>and dropping the real forks from the tracks.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Under the old matching this pushed the division Jaccard from ~0 to ~1.0, adding close to the full +0.1 to the score \u2014 even though none of the added nodes or edges correspond to a real cell or a real division. It's a pure scoring artifact; it does nothing for actual tracking quality.</p>\n<p><strong>The patch</strong> (now public in the repo) requires divisions to be genuine <strong>strongly-connected</strong> parent\u2192two-daughter structures matched within the 7 \u00b5m distance radius. The far-away synthetic forks now register as false positives instead of true positives, which removes the benefit and is why exploiting submissions will drop on re-score.</p>",
